<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mértola</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Myrtilis, Myrtilis Iulia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mértola is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alentejo">Alentejo</a> region of southern Portugal, on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a>. As Myrtilis Iulia it was a river port of Roman Lusitania, where in 440 the Suebic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rechila">Rechila</a> forced the Roman commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/censorius">Censorius</a> to surrender. Under Muslim rule it was for a time the capital of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a>, and its mosque, later converted into a church, survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mertola.jpg" alt="Mértola" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexey Komarov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%A9rtola,_Portugal_2019-11-10-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
